WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:07.210
A lending manager types the car loan policy into the chat, in plain English.

2
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:12.690
Who is declined and why, how the rate is set, when an officer reviews.

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.890
Not a line of rule syntax.

4
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.500
Then our app gets to work.

5
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.850
Our app reads it sentence by sentence and writes the rules.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.170
Then it checks them against the requirement:

7
00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:30.130
it finds one rule it got wrong, corrects it, and confirms every item.

8
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:36.290
Then it writes the specification: forty-two numbered items, each traced to its sentence.

9
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.450
Two entities, nineteen fields, fifteen rules.

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.790
The first one sets the interest rate from the credit score, the first tier that holds.

11
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.930
Spec Builder builds a DMN decision model from that specification, for Camunda 7.

12
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.470
Thirty-seven build steps, every one checked off.

13
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.990
Its tests run on Camunda, and all eighty-five pass.

14
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.130
Then a second check reads every requirement item against the rules.

15
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.610
Forty-two of forty-two, correct.

16
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:13.350
Our app's own report documents every entity, field and rule of the decision model it built,

17
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.610
the way your team would document one by hand.

18
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.830
It writes test cases from the requirement and runs them against the model.

19
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.930
Eighty-five tests, and all eighty-five pass.

20
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.880
Each one lists its inputs and the values it expects.

21
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.590
The decision model downloads as a standard DMN file, with its eighty-five tests.

22
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:40.490
The decision model opens right in the app.

23
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.170
Here, an application with five reasons to decline.

24
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.970
It is declined, and its rate, payment and debt-to-income ratio are worked out.

25
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.650
The rate comes from the credit score, the first tier that holds.

26
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.250
The amount financed is the price less the down payment.

27
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.810
Each decline rule adds its reason.

28
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.150
Here, the loan is larger than the car's value.

29
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:10.470
Then the decision: the first of decline, review or approve that holds.

30
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.510
The same case, on the standard DMN diagram.

31
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.970
Click a decision to see what it read, and what it decided for this case.

32
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.950
Then the tests run on three engines: C#, Drools and Camunda.

33
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.210
All eighty-five pass on each, with the same results.

34
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.870
Geny Systems.

35
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.870
Plain English business rules, built as DMN decision models, as C#,

36
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.150
or as InRule rule applications in your own environment, on your InRule licence.

37
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.500
Let's talk about your rule backlog.

